        "content": "Now let me solve the problem.\n\n**Part (1):** In △ABC, AB=3, BC=2√3, cosB=√3/3.\n\nBy law of cosines: AC² = AB² + BC² − 2·AB·BC·cosB = 9 + 12 − 2·3·2√3·(√3/3) = 21 − 12 = 9, so AC = 3.\n\nThen cosA = (AB² + AC² − BC²)/(2·AB·AC) = (9 + 9 − 12)/(2·3·3) = 6/18 = 1/3.\n\n**Part (2):** D on ray BA beyond A, DE∥BC, AE⊥AC, DE=√6, find CE.\n\nSet coordinates: A at origin, AC along x-axis with C=(3,0). Since cosA=1/3, sinA=2√2/3. B = 3(cosA, sinA) = (1, 2√2).\n\nD on ray BA beyond A: D = −t·(cosA, sinA) = (−t/3, −2√2 t/3) for t>0.\n\nDE∥BC: BC direction = C − B = (2, −2√2). So E = D + s(2, −2√2).\n\nAE⊥AC means AE is vertical, so E has x-coordinate 0: −t/3 + 2s = 0 → s = t/6.\n\nDE = |s|·|BC| = (t/6)·(2√3) = t√3/3 = √6 → t = 3√2.\n\nE = D + s(2,−2√2) with s = t/6 = √2/2: E = (−t/3 + 2s, −2√2t/3 − 2√2 s) = (−√2 + √2, −2√2·3√2/3 − 2√2·√2/2) = (0, −4 − 2) = (0, −6).\n\nCE = √((0−3)² + (−6−0)²) = √(9+36) = √45 = 3√5.",
